\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {(a)} How far a selected set sits from a random set of the same size, in held-out Cohen's $d$. Every rule that keeps the allocation's \emph  {concentration} reproduces the separation; only destroying concentration collapses it. Bars are means across the four cells at a budget of $N = 250$; whiskers show the range. \textbf  {(b)} Trained $S_\alpha $ at eight seeds for the three arms that hold concentration fixed and vary only direction; points are seeds, rules are arm means, the band is the $\pm 2\hat  {\sigma }$ bar. \emph  {Sources:} \texttt  {c1\_attribution.csv}; \texttt  {out/se/abc\_metrics.csv}.}}{7}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:thesis}{{2}{7}{\textbf {(a)} How far a selected set sits from a random set of the same size, in held-out Cohen's $d$. Every rule that keeps the allocation's \emph {concentration} reproduces the separation; only destroying concentration collapses it. Bars are means across the four cells at a budget of $N = 250$; whiskers show the range. \textbf {(b)} Trained $S_\alpha $ at eight seeds for the three arms that hold concentration fixed and vary only direction; points are seeds, rules are arm means, the band is the $\pm 2\hat {\sigma }$ bar. \emph {Sources:} \texttt {c1\_attribution.csv}; \texttt {out/se/abc\_metrics.csv}}{figure.2}{}}
